course: dictionary
lesson: 123
title: Adult in context
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word adult can be a noun or an adjective, so its job changes with the sentence. As a noun it names a person or thing: «Adults must come with children on the trip.» Here adults means grown-up people. As an adjective it describes something: «Adult tickets cost more.» Here adult tells you which kind of ticket.
explain: Use the words around it to find the meaning. «A butterfly's life cycle is egg, caterpillar, chrysalis and adult butterfly.» Adult means fully grown, the last stage of the life cycle. Animals and plants can be adult too, not only people.
explain: Clues can also tell you who to trust. «Leaving a group can feel scary, but trusted adults, like parents, teachers and youth workers, can help.» Trusted adults are grown-ups you can ask for help. Check your meaning by putting it back into the sentence.
fact: Adult is a noun (adult, adults) and an adjective (adult).
fact: An adult is a grown-up person, not a child.
fact: In many countries you are an adult from 18 years old.
fact: An adult animal or plant is one that is fully grown.
fact: «Trusted adults» means grown-ups you can ask for help, such as parents or teachers.
exercise: «Super glue sticks skin together in seconds, so only an adult uses it.» What does adult mean in this sentence? | A small child | *A grown-up person | A baby animal | A toy
exercise: «A tadpole grows into an adult frog.» What does adult mean here? | *A fully grown frog | A frog that is hungry | A frog that lives in a pond | A frog that is very small
exercise: In «adult tickets cost more», what kind of word is adult? | Adverb | *Adjective | Verb | Noun
test: «If you think a friend has stolen something, talk to them or a trusted adult.» Who does the sentence say you can ask for help? | *A trusted adult | Your pet | A stranger you have never met | A shop window
test: Which everyday slang word means doing boring grown-up jobs like paying bills? | Trusted | Ancient | *Adulting | Adult
exam: In an official form, which word could replace the phrase «persons aged 18 years or over»? | Pupil | *Adult | Tadpole | Child
